# Line endings, pinned per file type rather than left to each clone.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites every text
# file to CRLF on checkout. For install.sh that is fatal and the error does not
# say so: bash reads the shebang as "/usr/bin/env bash\r" and reports
# "bad interpreter: no such file or directory" for an interpreter that is
# plainly there. Same story for the hook scripts.
#
# Declaring it here means the answer no longer depends on how each person
# happens to have configured their git.

*           text=auto eol=lf

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf

# PowerShell and batch are read by Windows tools, which want CRLF. .ps1 in
# particular must stay ASCII-only as well -- see the header in install.ps1.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.mcpb      binary
